From 0cd50139c6cfcae6c74755bfe8ce656da9467bc3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 12:58:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Experience as a pinned road, auto-drifting field rail,
 longer intro

Three targeted changes; every other section, token and layout is unchanged.

Loader: timings only, no design change. Measured from navigation start,
NAV_MIN 900 -> 1400ms, MOUNT_MIN 560 -> 900ms, GREET_MS 640 -> 950ms,
EXIT_MS 700 -> 780ms, MAX_HOLD 2400 -> 3100ms. Module lines read out one per
250ms and the meter lands on 100 just before the panels lift, so the sequence
holds for about 2.35s instead of 1.5s.

Experience: the act now pins for n x 0.82 viewport-heights and every part of it
is driven by one eased --p in CSS, with no React state and no per-frame JS. The
road surface holds still while the mileposts travel so each one seats in the
marker ring at its station; the stations themselves travel through a window one
stretch of road tall, translating clear of it. The handover is travel rather
than a dissolve: opacity is asymmetric and station backgrounds are opaque, so an
arriving card covers the one it replaces instead of showing through it. The
first station greets in place at p = 0 and the last one holds. Where a station
cannot fit the window, or under prefers-reduced-motion, the same markup falls
back to a static rail.

Other experience: same cards, but the rail now drifts continuously and wraps
without a seam. The list repeats ceil(railWidth / loop) + 1 times and the drift
steps back exactly one set, with the period measured from getBoundingClientRect
so a rounded value cannot leave the wrap out of phase. The drift nudges the
container's own scrollLeft, so swipe, trackpad and arrow keys still work; it
holds on hover, on focus, for 1.6s after a manual nudge, off screen, on a hidden
tab, and entirely under prefers-reduced-motion.

Verified in Chromium at 1920x1080, 1440x900, 1280x800, 1024x768, 1024x720,
390x844 and 390x820, light and dark, English and Arabic, plus reduced motion:
the road has no empty stage at any of 23 scroll positions, every station reaches
full opacity without being clipped, zero dead steps, and the rail renders
identically at x and x + loop (mean pixel difference 0).

